.lua
book.chapter(1, "A language for building languages")
.endlua
.sh 2 "What MetaML gives you"
.pp
Meta-programming begins when a program manipulates another program's structure:
reading expressions, representing declarations, checking types, reorganizing
trees, or generating an output language. A useful meta-language must make those
structures pleasant to express without making their invariants invisible.
MetaML combines an ML-style functional core with algebraic data, inferred types,
modules, and a library of language-construction tools. You can start with small
transformations and grow them into a compiler pipeline embedded in an application.
.pp
The language has two host implementations. C++ clients include MetaML.hpp; D
clients import MetaML. Both accept the same intended language and share the
portable library sources under metalib. The host implementation is called a
dialect here; selecting it does not select a different syntax for your scripts.
The C interface is an adapter over the C++ interpreter, rather than a third
language implementation. The surrounding DSLtk toolkit supplies reusable
infrastructure for languages other than MetaML as well.
.pp
The book's title uses meta-programming in this practical sense. This MetaML does
not provide the quotation, escape, and run operators associated with some staged
languages with the same name. Instead, programs work with explicit data types,
generic AST values, parser libraries, and host interfaces. Being explicit about
the representation is valuable: it lets you decide what constitutes a valid
tree, which information a transformation preserves, and when evaluation occurs.
.sh 2 "One computation, several styles"
.pp
Named functions are a good starting point. A definition uses :=, application
uses whitespace, and a list pipeline passes data from left to right. The
following complete program computes the sum of three squares. Test.equal makes
the intended result executable rather than relying on an informal comment.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[square x := x * x
answer := [1, 2, 3] |> List.map square |> List.sum
Test.equal "sum of squares" answer 14]=], "A first complete program")
.endlua
.pp
A tacit function refers to numbered formal parameters. A section captures holes
in an operator expression. Composition combines functions before supplying their
input. These mechanisms describe ordinary typed functions; they do not create
three incompatible kinds of values. Prefer whichever notation exposes the
information a reader needs at that point. A short arithmetic helper is a good
place for numbered parameters; a substantial decision procedure usually needs
descriptive names.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[square := $1 * $1
increment := (+ 1)
transform := square >> increment
Test.equal "composed function" (transform 4) 17
Test.equal "explicit pipeline" (4 |> square |> increment) 17]=], "Tacit, sectional, and compositional forms")
.endlua
.pp
Modules provide a different scale of composition. A structure groups types and
values, a signature states an interface, and a functor builds a module from
another module. Together these let a library hide a representation while
retaining static guarantees about the operations clients can perform. They are
especially useful when several passes must agree on an abstract symbol or node
type. Chapters 16 through 18 develop that architectural level carefully.
.sh 2 "The route from source to result"
.pp
An interpreter does more than substitute names in text. It first recognizes
tokens, builds language-specific syntax, checks relationships between types,
then evaluates the checked program. A source span connects a failure at any of
these stages back to the original input. Treat this separation as a debugging
method: a malformed token, an invalid pattern, and a denied host operation need
different remedies even if they occur on the same source line.
.lua
book.chain({"Source text", "Tokens and syntax", "Checked program", "Values and effects"},
    "The interpreter's conceptual stages")
.endlua
.pp
Ordinary evaluation is eager. Calling a function evaluates its arguments before
executing the body. A closure delays its body until application, but does not
automatically delay its arguments. Module bodies initialize lazily when their
runtime values are first needed. Keeping these two statements separate avoids a
common mistake: assuming that a functional language must evaluate every
expression lazily. Effects such as printing make the difference observable.
.sh 2 "A working vocabulary for the journey"
.pp
A value is a result such as an integer, a record, or a function. An expression
computes a value. A binding associates a name with a value-producing definition.
A type constrains which operations fit together. An AST is a structured
representation of source rather than its original character sequence. A visitor
systematically interprets or transforms a tree. A primitive is a host operation
registered at the language boundary. These terms recur because a language tool
often has all of them at once, at different levels.
.pp
For example, an expression-language compiler written in MetaML has its own
expression values, while MetaML itself also has an internal expression AST.
The generic Ast supplied by ASTBuilder is another representation again. Do not
assume that a generic node tagged "add" is automatically a checked MetaML
addition. Your tool must assign the meaning. Chapter 31 demonstrates this
distinction by parsing a small input language, translating to a typed domain
model, simplifying it, evaluating it, and emitting another representation.
.sh 2 "How to use this book"
.pp
Read the first twenty chapters sequentially if ML-family languages are new to
you. If you already know functional programming, pay particular attention to
tacit parameters, typed juxtaposition, source layout, module initialization,
and the distinction between ordinary errors and Result values. Library chapters
then build toward the complete toolchain. The embedding chapters explain
lifetime and permission boundaries, and six dedicated chapters examine DSLtk.hpp
at the C++ level with corresponding D viewpoints.
.pp
Every complete MetaML listing is independent. Run it with the supplied CLI and
an explicit standard-library directory; the next chapter gives the commands.
The book checker starts fresh processes so definitions cannot accidentally leak
from earlier examples. Listings labelled pseudocode describe algorithms rather
than claiming runnable syntax. Descriptive EBNF excerpts explain selected forms
without pretending to replace the implementation's complete parser.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Start with MetaML.hpp, MetaML.d, metalib/prelude/mod.ml, and
metaml-cli/metaml.cpp. The compact metaml-manual directory is useful for
signature lookup alongside this longer treatment. As an exercise, change the
first program to sum only even squares and state its expected result before
running it. Then express the element transformation in named and tacit form.
The important observation is that notation changes while the function's input,
output, and testable contract remain the same.
